Method What it does <code>evaluate(&amp;argument.value)</code> the value of an argument; <code>ext::value_of</code> calls it <code>apply_lambda(argument, element)</code> an argument that is a function, applied to one value <code>apply_function(argument, values)</code> an argument that is a function, applied to several values <code>call(&amp;function, values, &amp;span)</code> a function value, applied to values: an element of a list of functions, for example <code>compare_values(&amp;a, &amp;b, &amp;span)</code> the order of two values, by the type's own <code>Ord</code> where it has one <code>equal_values(&amp;a, &amp;b, &amp;span)</code> whether two values are equal as <code>==</code> says <code>draw(&amp;span)</code> 64 bits from the evaluation's random seed, which <code>--seed</code> fixes <code>warn(diagnostic)</code> a diagnostic that does not fail the call <code>vault()</code> the vault the program runs against <p><code>call</code> differs from <code>apply_lambda</code> in what it is given. <code>apply_lambda</code> takes an argument of the call, still unevaluated. <code>call</code> takes a value that already is a function, which is what a list of functions holds once the list is evaluated:</p> <pre><code>let readers = ext::value_of(cx, features)?;          // a List of functions
for reader in elements(&amp;readers) {
    let feature = cx.call(reader, vec![row.clone()], &amp;span)?;
}
</code></pre> <p><code>draw</code> is how a function without a <code>seed</code> argument of its own stays reproducible: its draws follow the program's seed, so <code>hql --seed 7</code> repeats them. A function that draws this way is declared <code>effectful fn</code> (purity).</p>
